# Copy to .env and fill in. Never commit .env — it is gitignored, keep it that way.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Model provider  (required)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sarvam is the default. It is the only one of the three whose free ceilings are
# per MINUTE rather than per day (40 req/min on the starter tier), and its 128K
# context fits a requirements document alongside a site map — Groq refuses a
# request over 8,000 tokens outright, and Gemini allows 20 requests A DAY against
# a mission that spends 10-15.
#
# Get a key: https://dashboard.sarvam.ai
LLM_PROVIDER=sarvam
SARVAM_API_KEY=
SARVAM_MODEL=sarvam-105b

# SARVAM_MODEL choices:
#
#   sarvam-105b                128K context — the right shape for a PRD plus a map
#   sarvam-105b-conversations  32K, dialogue-tuned
#
# Both constrain structured output, so unlike GROQ_MODEL this can be changed
# without losing the planner's guarantee that it cannot emit an invalid action.
#
# How much of the completion budget the model may spend thinking before it writes
# any JSON: low | medium | high. Low by default — every call here is extraction
# against a schema, and reasoning tokens come out of the same budget as the
# output, so a plan that reasons at length truncates rather than improves.
# SARVAM_REASONING_EFFORT=low

# `npm run llm:check` reports which provider, model and decoding mode you are
# actually in — run it before a demo, not during one.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Groq  (alternative provider)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Only used when LLM_PROVIDER=groq, or when no SARVAM_API_KEY is set.
# Get a key: https://console.groq.com/keys
GROQ_API_KEY=
GROQ_MODEL=openai/gpt-oss-20b

# GROQ_MODEL must stay on a model whose structured output is *constrained* rather
# than best-effort, or the planner loses the guarantee that it cannot emit an
# invalid action. Only these three qualify:
#
#   openai/gpt-oss-20b     faster, cheaper, weaker on long prompts
#   openai/gpt-oss-120b    handles a requirements document plus a site map
#   qwen/qwen3.8-27b
#
# Free-tier ceilings, all of them real: 8,000 tokens per request, 8,000 per
# minute, 200,000 per day. They are why one mission takes minutes.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Gemini  (alternative provider)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Only used when LLM_PROVIDER=gemini, or when neither of the keys above is set.
# The daily free-tier limit of 20 requests is not enough to demonstrate with.
# Get a key: https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
GEMINI_API_KEY=
GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-2.5-flash

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Credentials for the application under test
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A stored plan holds only the NAME of each variable, never its value, so a
# password never reaches a file on disk, a prompt, or a log line. The crawler
# also uses these to get past a login form — without them it maps the sign-in
# page and nothing behind it.
#
# The values below are the bundled fixture's own credentials. They are not a
# secret: the fixture accepts exactly this pair and is served from this same
# process. Point them at something real to test a real application.
TEST_EMAIL=demo@example.com
TEST_PASSWORD=demo-password

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Server
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOST stays on loopback deliberately, so a development machine does not expose a
# browser-driving service to its network. The container sets HOST=0.0.0.0 itself;
# do not change this default to make a container work.
HOST=127.0.0.1
PORT=7860

# One run at a time. Each run drives a real browser at two viewports, so raising
# this on a small container trades a queue for an out-of-memory kill.
RUN_CONCURRENCY=1

# Where the database and captured artifacts are written. Kept separate from the
# repository's committed seed data, which is read-only.
# DATA_DIR=./data

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Orchestrator tuning  (optional — the defaults suit a free tier)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# How many times the orchestrator will go back and plan for coverage it is
# missing. Each round is another plan to record and run, so on a free tier a
# third plan pushes a mission past ten minutes of mostly waiting.
# MAX_REPLAN_ROUNDS=1

# Per-stage ceiling in milliseconds. Generous because rate limits mean a
# six-step plan is minutes of waiting, and a tight ceiling kills recordings that
# are progressing normally.
# STAGE_TIMEOUT_MS=600000
